"Moving to Texas: A Guide to Driver Licenses and IDs | Department of Public Safety (https://www.dps.texas.gov/section/driver-license/moving-texas-guide-driver-licenses-and-ids)\nciteturn15view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn2search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 58\nL0: Search Form Search\nL1: \nL2: [Input: What can we help you find?]\nL3: \nL4: [Button: Search]\nL5: \nL6: [Input] [Input]\nL7: \nL8: ## Change Text Size\nL9: \nL10: ^{-}A A ^{+}A\nL11: \nL12: ## Change Display Setting\nL13: \nL14: cite0†Default cite1†Text Only L15: \nL16: # Moving to Texas: A Guide to Driver Licenses and IDs\nL17: \nL18: September 22, 2020\nL19: ### New Texas Residents:\nL20:   * Initial Driving Privileges:  You can legally drive with a valid, unexpired driver license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canadian province, or qualifying country for up to 90-days after moving to Texas.\nL21:   * Transferring Out-of-State Licenses:  If you have a valid driver license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, or Canada, you must surrender your out-of-state license and are not required to take the knowledge or skills exams.\nL22:   * Evidence of Texas Vehicle Registration:  Before applying for a Texas driver license, ensure all your vehicles are registered with the Texas Department of Motor Vehicles.  Visit cite2†Texas Vehicle Registration†www.texas.gov for more information.\nL23:   * Reciprocity Agreements:  Texas has license reciprocity with France, Germany, South Korea, United Arab Emirates and Taiwan.  If you have a valid, unexpired driver license from one of these countries, you are not required to take the knowledge or skills exam unless you choose to keep your out-of-country license.\nL24:   * Translation Requirements:  Foreign licenses not in English or Spanish must be translated by a translation service or your consulate before arriving in Texas. Foreign marriage licenses not in English must be submitted with a certified English translation.\nL25: ### Waivers:\nL26: \nL27:   * Age 18 and Older:  If you hold a valid out-of-state driver license or license not expired over two years you are exempt from the knowledge and skills exams, as well as the ITD and adult driver education requirements.\nL28:   * Reciprocity Countries:  If you have a valid, unexpired driver license from France, South Korea, Germany, United Arab Emirates or Taiwan, the knowledge and skills exams may be waived.\nL29: ### Minors (Under age 18):\nL30:   * Knowledge Exam: If you have a valid driver license or learners license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, or Canadian province, you do not have to take the knowledge exam.\nL31:   * Surrendering Licenses:  Applicants who surrender a valid license/permit from another state will be issued the equivalent Texas version.\nL32:   * Non-U.S./Canada:  If you are under 18 from a country other than the U.S. or Canada, you must take and pass the knowledge exam.\nL33:   * Learners License Holding Period:  Texas law requires that anyone issued a Texas learner license must hold it for at least six months or until they are 18 years of age.\nL34:   * Skills Exam:  All applicants under age 18 must take the skills exam.\nL35: ### Learner Licenses:\nL36: \nL37:   * Transferring Learner Licenses:  If you have a valid, unexpired learner license from another U.S. state, U.S. territory, Canada, France, South Korea, Germany, United Arab Emirates or Taiwan, you do not have to take the knowledge exam.  You must still complete the Impact Texas Driver (ITD) course and pass the skills exam to advance to a driver provisional license.\nL38: ### Active Duty and Recently Discharged Military:\nL39: \nL40:   * Exemption Eligibility:  U.S. military personnel on active duty or individuals discharged within the last 90 days with an expired driver license from another state may be exempt from the knowledge and skills exams.\nL41:   * Requirements:  To qualify, applicants must present a valid military ID and provide proof of the issuing state's military extension policy.\nL42: ### North Atlantic Treaty Organization (NATO)\nL43: \nL44:   * Exemption:  Applicants who are members of a force or civilian component of a cite3†NATO country†www.nato.int and present validating orders are exempt from the knowledge and skills exams.\nL45: \nL46: ### Obtaining a Texas Identification (ID) card\nL47: \nL48:   * Requirements: New Texas residents who want to apply for a Texas ID card must meet all requirements, including proof of Texas residency and identification.\nL49: \nL50: \nL51: \nL52: ### Follow DPS\nL53: ### Keep Texas Safe\nL54: \nL55: cite4†Report Suspicious Activity†iwatchtx.org L56: \nL57: © 2026 Texas Department of Public Safety. PDF files require cite5†Adobe Reader†get.adobe.com or compatible.\n"
